# The Entropy Workshop's downloadable artifact and its checksum.
#
# -text means git stores and checks out these bytes exactly, with no
# end-of-line translation in either direction. That is not a preference here,
# it is a correctness requirement: the SHA-256 published beside the artifact
# describes specific bytes, and letting git rewrite them on commit or checkout
# made the published checksum describe a file nobody could obtain. Someone
# following the verify instructions got a mismatch and was right to stop.
docs/entropy-offline.html       -text
docs/entropy-offline.html.sha256 -text
*.sh text eol=lf

# Markdown is written on Windows and Linux both, and nothing in the build
# reads it, so its line endings are invisible until a diff shows a whole file
# rewritten. That happened once: an editing script wrote LICENSING-AUDIT.md
# back as CRLF with one stray CR, and with core.autocrlf left to decide, those
# bytes went into the blob as written -- 267 insertions and 266 deletions for
# a two-line change.
#
# Every tracked .md blob is LF today. This pins that, so the next edit made on
# a Windows checkout cannot quietly commit a different file.
*.md text eol=lf

# Licence and notice texts, which have no .md extension and so were not covered
# by the rule above. These are reproduced upstream documents: their bytes are
# the point, several are compared against served files by the build's notice
# check, and one is a licence this project grants under. Every one of these
# blobs is LF today, so this pins the current state rather than changing it --
# what it prevents is the same accident the Markdown rule prevents, an edit made
# on a Windows checkout quietly committing a different file.
LICENSE         text eol=lf
COPYING         text eol=lf
LICENSE-*.txt   text eol=lf
OFL-*.txt       text eol=lf
